Build Rockbox for the HiFi Walker H2

This tree builds for one player only: the HiFi Walker H2 / AIGO Eros Q native
(target 'erosqnative'). It cannot build bootloaders; the player needs the
official Rockbox bootloader.

1. Install the tools needed to build (Ubuntu/Debian; package names differ on
   other systems). libsdl2-dev is only needed for the simulator.

   $ sudo apt install build-essential git perl zip unzip wget flex bison \
         texinfo libsdl2-dev

2. Download the code.

   $ git clone https://github.com/dismal-ad/h2-rockbox.git
   $ cd h2-rockbox

3. Build the MIPS cross-compiler (mipsel-elf-gcc). This is needed once and
   takes a while. RBDEV_TARGET=i selects MIPS; RBDEV_PREFIX is where it is
   installed.

   $ RBDEV_PREFIX=$HOME/rockbox-toolchain RBDEV_TARGET=i tools/rockboxdev.sh

   Then put it in the PATH (add this line to ~/.bashrc to keep it):

   $ export PATH=$HOME/rockbox-toolchain/bin:$PATH
   $ which mipsel-elf-gcc

4. Create a build directory and configure it for the player. All generated
   files are written there.

   $ mkdir build
   $ cd build
   $ ../tools/configure --target=erosqnative --type=n

5. Build the firmware and pack it.

   $ make -j$(nproc)
   $ make zip

6. Unzip rockbox.zip to the root of the SD card (it contains the .rockbox
   folder), eject the card, reboot the player and *smile*. To update an
   existing install and keep your settings, unzip over it:

   $ unzip -o rockbox.zip -d /path/to/sdcard

Simulator

The simulator runs Rockbox in a window on the PC, with the same screens. It
needs libsdl2-dev but not the cross-compiler. Its SD card is the simdisk/
folder in the build directory: copy music there to test with.

   $ mkdir build-sim
   $ cd build-sim
   $ ../tools/configure --target=erosqnative --type=s
   $ make -j$(nproc)
   $ make install
   $ ./rockboxui

Keys: arrows = scroll wheel / previous / next, Enter = select (Centre),
Backspace = back, Insert = menu, PgUp/PgDn = volume, Esc = power.

Other build types: --type=a (advanced: logf, debug, ...) and --type=w (the
warble codec test tool). Use one build directory per build type.
